.TH FATSTRESS 1
.SH NAME
fatstress \- hammer a FAT file system and check what comes back
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fatstress
[
.BI -n " rounds"
]
[
.BI -f " files"
]
[
.BI -s " maxk"
]
.I dir
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fatstress
writes, reads back, renames and removes files in
.IR dir ,
which is normally a FAT file system served by
.IR dossrv (4),
and reports what did not survive.
Each round creates
.I files
files, checks every one of them, renames every one of them and
checks it again, tries a rename onto a name that already exists,
and removes half of them.
The options are:
.TP
.BI -n " rounds"
how many rounds to run; the default is 4
.TP
.BI -f " files"
how many files each round writes; the default is 12
.TP
.BI -s " maxk"
the largest file, in kilobytes; the default is 200
.PP
A file's contents are a function of a key that stays with the file
across a rename, so a file that comes back holding another file's
bytes is caught and not only one that comes back short.
The names are a mixture of long ones and 8.3 ones, because they take
different numbers of directory entries and only a long one can
straddle a sector, and the sizes are chosen so that a file of no
clusters, a file of one and a file of many are all covered.
.PP
The last line is
.B FATSTRESS-OK
or
.BR "FATSTRESS-FAIL n failures" ,
and each failure is reported as it happens.
.PP
.B /tests/dossrv-stress.sh
runs it on the build host against three file systems built with
.IR newfs_msdos :
FAT16 with 512-byte clusters, FAT32 with 512-byte clusters and a
34MB filler so that files land above cluster 65535, and FAT32 with
4KB clusters.
It then runs
.B "fsck_msdos -n"
over each image, which is the part that matters: reading the files
back through the same code that wrote them is not a check on that
code, and every FAT bug this found left an image that still mounted
and still listed.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/fatstress.b
.br
.B /tests/dossrv-stress.sh
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR dossrv (4)
.SH BUGS
It does not test concurrent writers, and it never crashes the server
in the middle of a write, so it says nothing about what a FAT looks
like after a power cut.
